3. Results and discussion
In this section, the results from the simulations performed for the numerical solution of the liquid oxygen flow in the Additively Manufactured (AM) cooling channel of the DemoP1 aerospike engine demonstrator designed by Pangea Aerospace are reported. Different versions of the two-equation k −ω turbulence model and roughness modelling approaches on the commercial CFD package are investigated for their performance on the prediction of the flow-field characteristics of the coolant flow in the AM cooling channel: a) the standard (STD) two-equation k − ω [32], the baseline (BSL) k − ω [33] and the k − ω shear stress transport (SST) [33] turbulence models using the standard roughness approach and, b) the two-equation k − ω shear stress transport (SST) turbulence model [33] using the
